# Line endings.
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Development happens on Windows; everything ships in a Linux container. A shell
# script written with CRLF endings has a shebang of `#!/bin/sh\r`, and the kernel
# looks for an interpreter literally named `sh\r`. The container then fails with:
#
#     exec /usr/local/bin/start-stack.sh: no such file or directory
#
# ...naming a file that plainly exists and is executable. It cost a build to find, and
# it would have cost another the first time anyone edited one of these on Windows.
#
# Marking them `text eol=lf` makes git normalise on checkout regardless of platform,
# so the fix is durable rather than a one-off conversion someone later undoes.

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Entrypoints and configs read by Linux tooling. Alertmanager, Prometheus and the
# collector all tolerate CRLF in YAML, but nginx and supervisord do not always, and a
# trailing \r inside a quoted value is invisible in review.
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
*.template  text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch.
*.jar       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
